by mathroc at 2023-12-09T11:29:41Z

I also implemented something at work, but made some different design choices, so just sharing.

I've handled string vs array of a single header differently: I made 2 attributes `#[FromFirstHeader]` (that can resolve to a non array scalar or a ValueObject (e.g. `\DateTimeImmutable`) & `#[FromHeader]` that resolves to `array<T>`

And when creating a DTO it's another attribute `#[FromHeaders]`. In my case, it resolves to DTO that have array properties

---

also, when resolving a single header to a int or float, I have some handmade rules to cast them, don't know if that would be controversial:

```php
[$value, $validType] = match ($type) {
    'int' => [(int) $value, (string) $value === (string) (int) $value],
    'float' => [(float) $value, (string) $value === (string) (float) $value],
    'string' => [(string) $value, true],
    default => throw new LogicException("FromHeader: Invalid type $type"),
};

if (!$validType) {
    throw new SomeException(
        "Header `$name` is not a valid `$type`.",
    );
}
````

---

and last point, I also have a `$constraints` on the attributes that are checked. can be useful for scalar since they can't have validation attributes on them

---

none of them are blockers I think, so I'll let you decide if there's something to pick from this. the only one I would be a bit disappointed if it was not included is the ability to deserialize a single header to a ValueObject

